---
// Where the models go wrong: signed error per buoy, and the share of each system's error that is a
// constant, correctable offset. All quoted figures come from the frozen data at build time.
import decayData from "../../../../public/data/surf-forecast/skill_decay.json";
import stationData from "../../../../public/data/surf-forecast/stations.json";
import { need, pick, signedFt } from "../../../lib/surf-forecast/findings";
import { BUOYS } from "../../../lib/surf-forecast/spots";

const H = "wave_height";
const LEAD = 3;
const all = (source: string) => pick(decayData.rows, "all", source, H, LEAD);
const at = (station: string, source: string) => need(pick(stationData.rows, station, source, H, LEAD).bias, `bias ${station} ${source}`);

const systems = [
	{ id: "ecmwf_wam", name: "ECMWF" },
	{ id: "ncep_gfswave016", name: "NOAA" },
	{ id: "gwam", name: "DWD" },
];
const split = systems.map((s) => {
	const r = all(s.id);
	const bias = need(r.bias, "bias");
	const scatter = need(r.centered_rmse, "scatter");
	return { ...s, bias, offsetShare: (bias * bias) / (bias * bias + scatter * scatter) };
});
const byName = Object.fromEntries(split.map((s) => [s.name, s]));
const closestToZero = [...split].sort((a, b) => Math.abs(a.bias) - Math.abs(b.bias))[0].name;

const stations = Object.keys(BUOYS);
const noaaLowCount = stations.filter((s) => at(s, "ncep_gfswave016") < 0).length;
const pauwela = at("51205", "ncep_gfswave016");
const ph = { e: at("51211", "ecmwf_wam"), n: at("51211", "ncep_gfswave016"), d: at("51211", "gwam") };
const scrippsAllHigh = ["ecmwf_wam", "ncep_gfswave016", "gwam"].every((m) => at("46254", m) > 0);
const scrippsDwd = at("46254", "gwam");
const sfFine = at("46237", "ncep_gfswave016");
const sfCoarse = at("46237", "ncep_gfswave025");
const pctOf = (x: number) => `${Math.round(x * 100)}%`;
---

<section id="bias" class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 py-16 md:px-12 md:py-20">
	<h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight md:text-3xl">Where the models go wrong</h2>
	<div class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		<p>
			Any error can be split exactly into a constant offset and the scatter around it. The offset is the part you can
			correct for once you know it ("this one always reads a foot small"); the scatter is the part nobody can. Split
			that way, the three systems are not just differently accurate. They are wrong in different directions.
		</p>
		<p>
			NOAA forecasts too small: {signedFt(byName.NOAA.bias)} ft on average three days out, too small at {noaaLowCount} of
			{stations.length} buoys, and {signedFt(pauwela)} ft at Pauwela on Maui's north shore. DWD forecasts too big, by
			{" "}{signedFt(byName.DWD.bias)} ft. {closestToZero} sits closest to zero, at {signedFt(byName[closestToZero].bias)}
			ft. For NOAA the constant offset is {pctOf(byName.NOAA.offsetShare)} of its squared error and for DWD
			{" "}{pctOf(byName.DWD.offsetShare)}: a real share of what makes them look worse is a consistent lean you could
			learn to read through. For ECMWF it is {pctOf(byName.ECMWF.offsetShare)}.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div id="fg-bias" class="mt-8">
		<div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3">
			<div data-fg-legend class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-2 text-sm text-ink"></div>
			<button type="button" data-vg-table-toggle class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-accent-blue" aria-pressed="false">
				View as table
			</button>
		</div>
		<div data-vg-chart class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"></div>
		<div data-vg-table class="mt-2 overflow-x-auto"></div>
		<p class="mt-3 font-mono text-[0.7rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			Average signed error in wave height, three days out. Each buoy is sorted by its systems' mean error, largest
			overforecast first. The grey bar is how far apart the systems are at that buoy.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="mt-8 max-w-[65ch] space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		<p>
			The buoys where every system overshoots are the sheltered ones. Pearl Harbor sits on Oahu's south shore, behind
			the island from the north swells that dominate the winter. ECMWF overforecasts it by {signedFt(ph.e)} ft, NOAA by
			{" "}{signedFt(ph.n)} ft and DWD, the coarsest, by {signedFt(ph.d)} ft.
			{
				scrippsAllHigh && (
					<>
						{" "}Scripps Nearshore, tucked into La Jolla, shows the same pattern, with DWD {signedFt(scrippsDwd)} ft too big.
					</>
				)
			}{" "}
			A global model on a grid tens of kilometres wide cannot see the island or headland doing the sheltering, so it
			delivers open-ocean swell to a buoy that never receives it. That was the island-shadowing hypothesis this project
			set out to test, and the buoys support it.
		</p>
		<p>
			A finer grid is not automatically the cure. At the San Francisco Bar, NOAA's finer output takes its sample near
			the Golden Gate, at a point the model itself treats as sheltered, and reads {signedFt(sfFine)} ft. Its coarser
			output samples farther offshore and reads {signedFt(sfCoarse)} ft. Where a model takes its sample matters as much
			as how good the model is.
		</p>
	</div>

	<script src="../../../scripts/surf-forecast/bias-chart.ts"></script>
</section>
